AU  - Zhou, H.
TI  - Hazard-responsive digital twin for weather-driven urban resilience and affordability
PY  - 2026
T2  - Sustainable Cities and Society
VL  - 144
C7  - 107413
DO  - 10.1016/j.scs.2026.107413
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105036725895?origin=resultslist
AD  - Buildings and Transportation Science Division, Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Oak Ridge, 37831, TN, United States
AD  - Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Tennessee, Knoxville, 37902, TN, United States
AB  - Compounding weather hazards, such as wildfire-induced outages and urban heatwaves, challenge the reliability, affordability, and safety of urban services. We present a Hazard-Responsive Digital Twin (H-RDT) that combines physics-informed neural network modeling, multimodal data fusion, affiliation learning, and affordability-aware risk analytics for urban-scale response. In a screening-level synthetic district with diverse building archetypes and populations, a simulated wildfire–outage–heatwave cascade shows that H-RDT maintains stable indoor temperature predictions (∼31–33 °C peaks) under partial sensor loss, while reproducing outage-driven surges and recovery. The reinforcement learning based fusion module adaptively reweights IoT, UAV, and satellite inputs to sustain spatiotemporal coverage, and the affordability-adjusted mapping isolates high-vulnerability clusters such as schools, clinics, low-income housing. Prospective interventions, i.e., preemptive cooling-center activation and microgrid sharing, reduce population-weighted thermal risk by 11–13%, shrink the 95th-percentile (tail) risk by 7–17%, and cut overheating hours by up to 9%. Beyond this synthetic demonstration, the framework establishes a transferable pathway for real-city implementation by linking hazard modeling, reliability-focused analytics, and decision intelligence for resilience planning. © 2026
