TI  - Scheme and algorithm designs for optical fiber sensors based configuration inversion of umbilical cables in deep-water
PY  - 2025
T2  - Ocean Engineering
VL  - 332
C7  - 121363
DO  - 10.1016/j.oceaneng.2025.121363
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105003906922?origin=resultslist
AD  - College of Engineering, Ocean University of China, Shandong Province, Qingdao, 266404, China
AB  - In the realm of marine resource exploration and exploitation, umbilical cables are indispensable as the exclusive pathway for the transmission of energy and signals towards underwater systems. The safe operation of these cables is paramount to the safety and reliability of the entire explorat ion or exploitation equipment. The predominant safe risks of umbilical cables stem from suboptimal configuration, which makes the real-time configuration inversion of the cables very necessary. Despite the importance, due to limitations in model accuracy and computational complexity, traditional mechanical analysis methods are unable to provide reliable real-time simulations of umbilical cables' configuration. Building upon the pioneering digital twinning concept and advanced optical fiber sensing technique, this work introduced novel scheme and algorithm to solve the problem of the real-time configuration inversion of umbilical cables. In terms of the scheme design, this work employed distributed strain-sensing optical fibers as the sensing elements and selects the Φ-OTDR as the demodulation module. Furthermore, an innovative arrangement strategy for the strain-sensing optical fibers is proposed. In terms of the algorithm design, the work refined the algorithm based on the Frenet formulas and designed some ancillary devices to augment the computational precision and efficiency. This work delineated the framework for the real-time configuration inversion of umbilical cables in deep-water, which established a robust ground for achieving digital twin of umbilical cables’ dynamic responses. © 2025 Elsevier Ltd
