AD  - Key Laboratory of Emerging Work Safety Risk Identification and Prevention, Ministry of Emergency Management, Beijing, 100054, China
AB  - Digital twin technology is increasingly used to support proactive safety management, but existing studies span diverse domains and provide limited direct evidence for process-industry loss prevention. This study adopts a two-level bibliometric design. A broad corpus of 1526 records on digital twin-enabled safety applications was retrieved from the Web of Science Core Collection using bibliometric methods. A process-safety-specific corpus of 198 records was then constructed to support a focused analysis of process safety and loss prevention. The broad corpus shows that digital twin safety research is distributed across construction safety, structural health monitoring, robotics, vehicle systems, battery systems, smart manufacturing, and real-time monitoring. The focused corpus is more closely associated with high-risk process and energy systems, with sources in nuclear energy, process safety, reliability engineering, and interdisciplinary engineering. Locally cited documents highlight digital twins for oil and gas, digitalization and Industry 4.0 in process safety, and autonomous monitoring of advanced reactors. Keyword results identify visible themes including digital twin frameworks, simulation, optimization, artificial intelligence, machine learning, physical–virtual integration, and industrial process systems. These findings, from a bibliometric perspective, show several directions that could be considered for priority in loss prevention for the process industries: model validation, reliable physical-virtual integration, sensor data quality, AI-assisted diagnosis and optimization, and closer integration with established process-safety methods. These efforts aim to advance both the theoretical foundation and practical implementation of digital twin-enabled safety management, supporting the safe and sustainable development of industrial systems. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
