AB  - Non-metallic pipes such as polyethylene (PE), polypropylene (PP), and glass fiber reinforced composites have been widely applied in oil and gas transportation, municipal construction, and other fields due to their light weight and corrosion resistance, and they show significant potential for hydrogen pipeline applications. Butt fusion welding and electrofusion welding are the core joining methods, and the reliability of the welded joints directly determines engineering safety, making related technical research a key focus. Based on a comprehensive review of the literature with bibliometric methods, this study systematically analyzes the research landscape and technological evolution in the field of non-metallic pipe welding, with a particular focus on simulation technologies. The results indicate that simulation has become a core technical approach, forming five major research directions: thermal and flow field simulation, stress and defect simulation, material micro-performance, multi-field coupling, and process optimization. Mainstream software platforms such as Abaqus, COMSOL, and ANSYS are widely used to support these efforts. Future development trends point toward intelligent simulation, enhanced multi-field coupling, and the integration of digital twin technologies. By combining a systematic review of the literature with in-depth technical analysis, this study clarifies the application status, technical bottlenecks, and optimization pathways of simulation technologies, providing valuable references for engineering practice and technological innovation in the field of non-metallic pipe welding. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer-Verlag London Ltd., part of Springer Nature 2026.
