AD  - Department of Civil Engineering, Sharif University of Technology, Azadi Ave., P.O. Box, 11155-1639, Tehran, Iran
AB  - Current construction safety protocols often stagnate by treating human error as a stochastic variable rather than a predictable, mechanistically driven parameter. Addressing this critical gap, this study introduces the Neuro-Cognitive Safety Framework (NCSF), a novel interdisciplinary paradigm that bridges environmental multi-physics stressors with worker neurobiology. Anchored by a Traceability Dictionary of over 150 empirically validated concepts and a hierarchical Level of Detail (LOD) architecture, the NCSF operationalizes Cognitive Load Theory. The framework’s core mechanistic concepts were empirically validated using an experimental Electroencephalography (EEG) and n-back dataset (N = 16). Results demonstrated significant behavioral degradation under peak cognitive load (accuracy dropping to 69.6%), which was preceded by predictive neuro-spectral markers, including parietal theta (∼4–6 Hz) enhancement and frontal alpha (∼9–11 Hz) suppression (p ' 0.05). Building upon this empirical foundation, a predictive Agent-Based Model (ABM) was developed to simulate how environmental stressors, specifically thermal and acoustic loads, non-linearly accelerate “Neural Resource Competition.” The simulation successfully identified a critical acoustic “Cognitive Tipping Point” around 73 dB. Crucially, rather than assuming a uniform group limit, the model captured profound inter-individual variability. Beyond these personalized thresholds, prefrontal executive control is depleted, predisposing workers to a “Habit Switch” where they regress to amygdala-driven reflexive reactions. Ultimately, by translating these specific neuro-cognitive parameters into a predictive computational domain, the NCSF provides the foundational logic for Industry 5.0 applications, such as Neuro-Cognitive Digital Twins (NCDTs), enabling a paradigm shift from reactive compliance to proactive, individualized safety engineering. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
