AB  - System-level thermo-mechanical states are essential for safety assessment and operational control of high-temperature energy equipment. However, field measurements are extremely sparse and confined to accessible components, while numerous time-dependent thermo-mechanical boundary conditions are unavailable. Inferring full-field states in real-time from highly local observations without explicitly identifying high-dimensional boundary conditions therefore remains a major challenge for digital-twin modeling. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a sparse-observation- and sparse-physics-informed system-level digital twin (SOPI-SDT), for real-time inverse inference of high-dimensional thermo-mechanical states. A concatenate-then-extract strategy constructs a unified latent space representing both component responses and inter-component coupling. Local sparse sensor observations and governing equations enforced on selected sparse elements are then imposed on the shared latent state, enabling direct low-dimensional inverse inference. Unlike data-driven observation-to-state mappings and conventional iterative inversion, the method bypasses explicit identification of high-dimensional time-dependent boundary conditions and avoids repeated forward-solver calls in an outer loop. The framework is applied to a high-temperature turbine cylinder system with approximately 3.3 million finite elements and 257 time-dependent boundary conditions. Using one artificial startup condition for offline construction, one inner-casing temperature signal, and rotor speed, SOPI-SDT reconstructs temperature, displacement, and stress fields for four industrial startups recorded from 2021 to 2025. Independent outer-casing sensors show errors below 2%. The average online cost is about 1.2 s per time step, corresponding to roughly five orders of magnitude acceleration. These results demonstrate accurate, real-time cross-component state inference under sparse sensing and incomplete boundary information. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd.
