VL  - 90
C7  - 112340
DO  - 10.1016/j.istruc.2026.112340
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105041561611?origin=resultslist
AD  - Zhejiang Institute of Communications, Zhejiang, Hangzhou, 311112, China
AD  - Faculty of Maritime and Transportation, Ningbo University, Ningbo, 315832, China
AD  - Zhejiang University, Hangzhou, 310058, China
AB  - Near-coast utility tunnels constructed in soft-soil environments are vulnerable to long-term settlement accumulation and structural response evolution, posing challenges for serviceability assessment and operational safety management. Existing studies generally focus on either deformation monitoring or structural response prediction, while integrated forecasting frameworks for heterogeneous monitoring data remain limited. This study proposes a dual-stream monitoring and predictive framework for operational-phase assessment of near-coast utility tunnels. Hydrostatic leveling system (HLS) measurements are employed to characterize vertical deformation and are modeled using the AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA) approach after stationarity diagnosis and differencing treatment. Meanwhile, strain-monitoring data are represented as a weighted sensor network and analyzed using a spatiotemporal graph neural network (STGNN) capable of capturing nonlinear temporal evolution and spatial coupling effects among monitoring locations. To ensure realistic operational evaluation, a rolling-origin forecasting strategy with missing-data masking and round-wise normalization is adopted. Results indicate that the ARIMA model achieved an RMSE of 0.62 mm and an R² value of 0.93 for one-day deformation forecasting, demonstrating strong short-term predictive capability. For strain-response prediction, the weighted STGNN achieved a 30-day forecasting RMSE of 0.025 mm and maintained a Pearson correlation coefficient exceeding 0.94. Comparative analyses show that the proposed multi-indicator STGNN outperformed single-indicator forecasting models by reducing prediction errors by approximately 15–20% for long-horizon forecasts. Ablation experiments further reveal that removing graph-based spatial coupling increased forecasting errors by 12.8%, confirming the importance of explicitly modeling inter-sensor dependencies. The proposed framework provides an effective and reproducible solution for structural prognosis, early warning, and maintenance planning of underground utility tunnels and offers a practical foundation for future digital-twin-driven infrastructure management systems. © 2026 Institution of Structural Engineers. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
