AD  - Department of Occupational Therapy, Department of Rehabilitation Science, Inje University, Gimhae-si, South Korea
AD  - HOPE Parent Training Center, Seoul, South Korea
AB  - Background: Dominant frameworks in transgender health, particularly Minority Stress Theory, presume an individualist subject for whom “outness” is a primary pathway to well-being. This model often fails to capture the experience of transition in South Korea, where selfhood is tethered to Neo-Confucian lineage and gender is policed by rigid administrative binaries. Aim: This study investigates the lived experience of transgender women in South Korea to operationalize how hypervigilance functions within a high-surveillance, collectivist society. Methods: Using Giorgi’s descriptive phenomenological method, in-depth interviews were conducted with six transgender women (ages mid-20s to early 40s). Data were analyzed in Korean to preserve indigenous sociolinguistic meanings. Results: Transition emerged not as a linear trajectory of self-actualization, but as involving ongoing negotiations of safety, identity, and social conditions, organized by four interrelated structures: (1) a Confucian Filial Gender Regime that frames gender variance as “moral injury” to the family; (2) Administrative Violence via the Resident Registration Number (RRN), which enforces binary citizenship; (3) Nunchi as Safety Labor, describing the sustained, face-based monitoring required to manage visibility; and (4) Parallel Survival Infrastructures (digital “shadow clinics”) constructed to navigate medical exclusion. Conclusion: In the Korean context, minority stress is relationally distributed and administratively enforced. We describe nunchi as a form of safety labor within a culturally specific context, highlighting how hypervigilance is performed as pro-social labor to maintain family “face” (chemyon). Interventions must move beyond individual affirmation to address structural determinants of health, including the RRN system and insurance inequity. © 2026 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
