AD  - China Yajiang Group Co., Ltd., Beijing, 100038, China
AB  - Structural health monitoring of high earth-rock dams is essential for ensuring operational safety under complex hydro-mechanical conditions. However, existing digital twin approaches mainly focus on state prediction and lack capabilities for intelligent fault reasoning and engineering decision-making. To address this issue, this study proposes a digital twin-driven intelligent fault diagnosis framework integrated with Large Language Models (LLMs). Multi-source monitoring data, finite element simulations, and transfer learning are combined to construct multi-physics digital twins of deformation, seepage, and stress fields. A robust gradient estimation method and a real-time slope stability model are developed to identify critical failure indicators, including seepage anomalies, cracking risks, hydraulic fracturing, and slope instability. Furthermore, a data semanticization engine is introduced to transform numerical fault information into engineering-oriented natural-language descriptions, which are subsequently analyzed by a dam-specific LLM for root-cause reasoning and mitigation recommendation generation. The proposed framework is validated using a 186 m-high core-wall rockfill dam with long-term monitoring data. The results demonstrate that the method can accurately reconstruct multi-physics fields, effectively identify potential structural risks, and provide interpretable decision-support information, showing strong potential for intelligent safety management of large hydraulic infrastructures. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
