AD  - Yunnan Open University, Yunnan Vocational And Technical College National Defense Industry, Yunnan, Kunming, 650500, China
AD  - Yunnan Minzu University, Yunnan Provincial Department Of Education Engineering, Research Center For Building Digital And Energy Saving Technology, Yunnan, Kunming, 650504, China
AB  - Earthquakes pose severe threats to urban safety. Traditional prediction methods fail to fully quantify seismic uncertainties and address low-probability, high-impact 'long-tail risks'. This paper proposes a partially validated probabilistic urban seismic damage prediction framework integrating large-scale scenario ensembles and digital twins. The framework is conceptually designed with four layers: data fusion, digital twin modeling, scenario ensemble simulation, and probabilistic assessment. Due to current data and computational constraints, empirical validation is limited to two core modules - the PI-GAN ground motion generation module and the probabilistic damage cloud module - using the 2021 Yangbi Ms6.4 earthquake (single event, single region). The remaining two modules (infrastructure cascading failure and social behavior modeling) are presented as conceptual designs requiring future validation. The validation results show that the Physics-Informed Generative Adversarial Network (PI-GAN) generates ground motions with ≤ 5 % PGA error and ≤ 10 % response spectrum error, while the probabilistic damage cloud module achieves 77.7% overall classification accuracy (with 79.5% recall, 87.3% precision, and 83.2% F1-score) and 89.3% structure-damage matching degree. The framework enables a shift from empirical judgment to data-driven pre-simulation, providing a partially validated and technically feasible solution for improving the accuracy and comprehensiveness of urban seismic risk assessment, while clearly acknowledging that full-chain validation - particularly for extreme M≥ 7.0 scenarios and unvalidated modules - remains as future work.  © 2013 IEEE.
