AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is increasingly being explored as a decision-support layer in process safety across chemical, energy, and manufacturing industries. Conventional safety approaches, often based on deterministic analyses, retrospective incident reviews, and static risk metrics, can be limited in highly interconnected and dynamically changing industrial systems. This review provides a structured qualitative synthesis of literature on AI applications relevant to process safety in high-risk industrial environments, with emphasis on machine learning (ML), deep learning (DL), surrogate modeling, and safety-oriented digital twins for hazard identification, dynamic risk assessment, fault diagnosis, predictive maintenance, emergency response, and safety management. Rather than proposing a new technical method, the review examines the field through an assurance-oriented lens, focusing on interpretability, uncertainty awareness, lifecycle validation, data governance, cybersecurity, and human–AI interaction as conditions for credible deployment in safety-critical settings. The review also highlights the relevance of AI-enabled process safety to Sustainable Development Goals by supporting a safer industry, worker protection, resilient infrastructure, and reduced environmental risk. Overall, the review emphasizes that the future value of AI in process safety depends not only on predictive performance but also on validation quality, transparency, governance, and human-supervised deployment. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd
