AB  - Wind turbine concrete towers are frequently subjected to complex stress states involving compression, bending, shear, and torsion during normal operation. To investigate the failure mechanisms and root causes of vertical joint degradation under bending-shear loads, this study performs a comprehensive failure analysis through combined experimental and computational approaches. Four vertical joint specimens were designed for push tests, followed by a systematic analysis of their failure modes and load-displacement curves. The results reveal that interface debonding between the epoxy and concrete, together with shear failure within the concrete layers, constitutes the primary failure mechanism at the joint interface. Variations in arc bolt pretension force and eccentric distance significantly influence the transition of failure modes, underscoring their roles as critical factors inducing joint failure. Finite element (FE) simulations incorporating detailed material characterisation and digital twin modelling were conducted to validate the experimental observations. The FE model accurately reproduced the load-displacement responses and failure modes, enabling parametric studies on the effects of bolt quantity, pretension force, concrete strength, joint length, and eccentric distance on shear capacity. This analysis establishes a solid foundation for understanding the shear resistance mechanism and developing preventive measures against joint failure. Based on these insights, a novel design method is proposed to enhance structural integrity. The method’s shear capacity predictions exhibit excellent agreement with the experimental results (average ratio = 1.004, CoV = 0.105), demonstrating its effectiveness for failure prevention in practical engineering. This study contributes to the broader field of engineering failure analysis by integrating experimental characterisation, computational modelling, and preventive design strategies for renewable energy infrastructure. © 2026 Institution of Structural Engineers. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
