AD  - Zhengzhou University, School of Management Engineering, Zhengzhou, 450000, China
AB  - Urban transport systems are increasingly susceptible to cascading failures, necessitating advanced methods for risk identification and mitigation. This paper presents a multi-modal risk embedding framework integrating ℓ2,1-regularized feature selection, spatiotemporal transformer encoding, and spatially-constrained spectral clustering for assessing vulnerability and structuring resilient communities within the transport networks of Singapore and Beijing. By fusing data from urban sensor networks - including traffic flow, structural health, and environmental metrics - into a unified 460-dimensional representation, the framework identifies and classifies cross-modal vulnerability patterns across structural, operational, and environmental dimensions. A core contribution is a novel spatially-constrained community detection algorithm that organizes the network into geographically contiguous resilient communities, enforced by a geographical shortest-path decay penalty to ensure operational actionability. The framework was trained using calibrated digital twin simulations generating one million synthetic disaster scenarios, and validated through retrospective analysis of 124 historical incidents across both cities. On real-world datasets spanning seven regional transport sub-networks, the framework achieves a macro-averaged F1 score of 0.964 in cross-modal risk classification, outperforming the best baseline (0.901), and identifies previously overlooked cross-modal vulnerability couplings (e.g., between metro lines and expressways). Furthermore, hardening the top 2% of AI-identified inter-community bridge nodes reduces systemic failure spread by 34.2%, compared to 18.6% for conventional betweenness centrality-based selection. This study provides a quantitatively validated, data-driven foundation for proactive infrastructure hardening and resilience management in high-density urban environments.  © 2013 IEEE.
