TI  - Autonomous control systems for technological processes in the field of hydrogen energy
PY  - 2026
T2  - International Journal of Hydrogen Energy
VL  - 203
C7  - 153076
DO  - 10.1016/j.ijhydene.2025.153076
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105026859308?origin=resultslist
AD  - Novosibirsk State Technical University, Marx Avenue, 20, Novosibirsk, 630073, Russian Federation
AB  - Hydrogen energy is emerging as a cornerstone of the global energy transition, demanding the development of autonomous, safe, and intelligent control systems for complex infrastructure. In this context, digital twin (DT) technology offers a transformative approach to monitoring, diagnosing, and optimizing the operation of hydrogen-based and hydroelectric systems. This paper presents a comprehensive methodology for implementing DT algorithms within relay protection and automation (RPA) frameworks, with direct integration into SCADA environments. The proposed algorithms enable real-time identification and localization of equipment faults by analyzing analog and discrete signals, significantly enhancing operational reliability and decision-making speed. The study focuses on phase-to-phase and single-phase fault scenarios in generator stator windings, demonstrating how DT logic can simulate and interpret emergency conditions with high precision. These solutions are particularly relevant for hydrogen energy facilities, where rapid fault detection and autonomous response are critical to safety and efficiency. The research builds upon historical experience from the hydrogen launch complex “Energia-Buran,” highlighting the evolution from expert-driven diagnostics to formalized digital models. By extending DT capabilities to hydrogen infrastructure—such as electrolyzers, fuel cell systems, and refueling stations—the presented approach contributes to the creation of next-generation intelligent platforms for hydrogen energy management. Hydrogen energy is a key area in solving the climate agenda. The energy transition will require the creation of new powerful, safe and reliable energy systems. Solving this problem will require mathematical models of digital twins. They not only ensure the reliable operation of the originals, but also predict their economic attractiveness. Issues of using digital twin technology are currently relevant in various areas of economic development. Electric power industry is no exception. Electric power industry is an industry with a complex structure. People are not always able to quickly process a huge amount of data, identify dependencies and make the best decision, so without digital technologies it is impossible to compete in modern realities. The article discusses the development and application of algorithms for determining types of damage to main equipment based on digital twin technology. Determination of the damage mode is based on the method of analyzing analog and discrete signals present in the technological data transmission network. As a result of the research, algorithms for a digital twin of relay protection and automation at an operating hydroelectric power station were developed, tested and implemented. The digital twin is aimed at reducing the time for detecting and localizing faults and damage to main equipment, as well as increasing information content about the operating modes of relay protection and automation devices. The results obtained can be implemented into an existing automated control system for hydroelectric power plants to improve the decision-making process. Flexible configuration allows not only to adapt algorithms to any equipment, but also to expand their functionality. The developed digital twin algorithms were successfully implemented within the SCADA system of an operational hydroelectric power station and validated through simulated fault scenarios. A comparison between emergency oscillogram parameters and the algorithm outputs demonstrated full consistency, confirming their reliability and applicability in real-world conditions. The resulting data include fault type, peak current and voltage values, timing characteristics, and recommended control actions. The proposed methodology is scalable across a wide range of hydrogen energy facilities, including electrolyzers, fuel cell systems, hydrogen turbines, and refueling stations. In environments without permanent personnel, digital twins enable autonomous decision-making, enhance operational safety, and integrate intelligent functions into existing control systems. This is particularly critical for hydrogen infrastructure, where high equipment sensitivity demands precise diagnostics and rapid response. Thus, the presented work contributes to the advancement of digital control technologies in hydrogen energy, combining engineering rigor, algorithmic precision, and a strategic focus on sustainable development. The application of digital twins facilitates a shift from reactive to proactive management, forming the foundation for next-generation intelligent platforms capable of ensuring reliable and safe operation of hydrogen infrastructure in the context of the global energy transition. © 2025
