UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105046855963?origin=resultslist
AD  - Civil Engineering and Construction Engineering Technology/Louisiana Tech University, Ruston, United States
AB  - Modern monitoring systems in structural health monitoring (SHM) and transportation generate continuous streams of heterogeneous, high-resolution data from distributed sensor networks. These data capture complex spatiotemporal interactions across multiple modes, including time, space, operating conditions, and environmental variables. High-order tensor representations provide a natural framework for preserving this multi-way structure, and tensor decompositions such as CANDECOMP/PARAFAC (CP), Tucker, and Tensor Train (TT) have been widely used for imputation, reconstruction, anomaly detection, and forecasting. However, most existing tensor decomposition methods are formulated in a batch setting, requiring repeated refitting as new data become available, which limits their applicability for real-time monitoring. This paper develops an Online Tensor Train (OTT) framework for incremental tensor decomposition of streaming infrastructure data. The proposed approach updates tensor representations sequentially as new observations arrive, reducing the need for full batch retraining and substantially lowering cumulative computational cost. The framework is evaluated on benchmark datasets from SHM, using the Z24 Bridge dataset, and transportation, using the Caltrans PeMS dataset, under a unified preprocessing and evaluation protocol. Results show that OTT achieves reconstruction accuracy comparable to batch CP and batch TT methods, with a modest accuracy gap relative to Tucker in some cases, while providing orders-of-magnitude reductions in per-update and cumulative runtime. OTT also preserves the compact memory characteristics of TT and supports residual-based anomaly and incident detection in streaming settings. These findings demonstrate that online tensor representations offer a practical and scalable solution for real-time infrastructure monitoring, particularly when continuous data ingestion, rapid updating, and computational efficiency are essential. This work establishes a foundation for integrating tensor decomposition with streaming analytics in digital-twin-enabled infrastructure monitoring and intelligent transportation systems. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
