AB  - High-fidelity digital twins are essential for infrastructure life cycle management, yet their development is hindered by the inherent conflict between reconstruction fidelity and data acquisition efficiency. Conventional automated flights often fail to capture complex substructures, while manual inspections are plagued by high costs and irreproducibility. To address this challenge, we propose an autonomous framework centered on a novel continuous sampling space initialization method that generates safety-constrained viewpoints tailored to bridge geometry. Within this space, we formulate a joint objective function that combines multiview stereo (MVS)-based quality (FQ) and path efficiency (FE). A particle swarm optimizer (PSO) is employed to extract a compact set of optimal viewpoints, which are subsequently sequenced into a deployable trajectory using a TSP solver. Experiments on real-world bridges establish a new benchmark: our method achieves centimeter-level accuracy with a mean error of 2.2 cm and improves reconstruction completeness by 20.3% compared to expert manual pilots. By surpassing human expertise in precision and consistency, this work provides a scalable, engineering-grade solution for the autonomous digitization of complex infrastructure.  © 2008-2012 IEEE.
