AB  - The exploitation of low-altitude airspace in urban areas has given rise to the concept of low-altitude economy (LAE). However, urban aviation may face safety challenges due to the complex environment caused by dense buildings. Therefore, a customized infrastructure that provides ubiquitous and reliable communication in the three-dimensional (3D) urban region is essential for efficient aircraft orchestration. This necessitates global and accurate radio environment information acquisition. As one of the promising technologies for the sixth generation (6G) system, channel knowledge map (CKM) can provide a priori location-specific channel knowledge for further communication performance improvement. Toward this end, we propose an end-to-end digital twin (DT) assisted 3D CKM inference and multi-type base station (BS) deployment optimization framework. This framework offers virtual replicas of wireless information by constructing channel gain map (CGM), which is then utilized for quality-of-service (QoS) analysis and BS deployment optimization. Specifically, the seamless 3D CGM can be rapidly inferred by leveraging a 3D conditional generative adversarial network (3D-CGAN) solely based on the BS coordinate without additional measurements. In addition, the deployment optimization for different types of BSs in the complex environment is investigated leveraging the channel information provided by the generated CGMs. The formulated black-box optimization problem is efficiently solved by the proposed algorithm based on Bayesian optimization. Simulation results demonstrate that the CGMs inferred by the proposed scheme outperform benchmarks by more accurately characterizing 3D radio propagation environments. Furthermore, the proposed algorithm effectively obtains optimized BS locations while accommodating deployment constraints for various BS types.  © 2026 IEEE.
