AB  - Traffic simulation technologies are now an essential tool in traffic safety research, as they enable complex real-world scenarios to be reproduced in a safe and controllable virtual environment. With the growing attention to mixed traffic and human-vehicle interaction, higher requirements have been placed on real-time interaction and behavioral fidelity across diverse road users. However, most existing platforms still center on a single road-user type, while other participants are often represented as scripted agents, which makes it difficult to capture genuine human reactions and interaction feedback. To address these limitations, we propose a high-fidelity, multi-road-user traffic behavior simulation system, the Multi-Road-User Interaction Simulation Platform (MRUISP). MRUISP consists of a hardware layer and a software layer. The hardware layer includes a driving simulator, a pedestrian simulator, a bicycle simulator, and an E-Bike Simulator. The software layer is built on Unity and integrates these simulators, enabling multiple users to operate different agents through their respective devices and interact within the same shared virtual scene in real time. Compared with existing systems, MRUISP offers stronger human-in-the-loop realism, broader participant inclusion, and greater extensibility. The platform's feasibility is evaluated through an initial case study involving a left-turn conflict at a reconstructed urban intersection, where simulated behavioral patterns are qualitatively consistent with real-world mixed-traffic observations. These results provide preliminary evidence supporting MRUISP's potential for behavioral analysis, risk assessment, and multi-road-user interaction research. In summary, MRUISP presents a validated prototype architecture that addresses a critical gap by enabling synchronized, embodied interaction among heterogeneous road users, and offers a scalable foundation for future research in traffic safety, infrastructure design, and digital-twin simulation. © 2026 Elsevier B.V. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
