TI  - Reinforcement learning-based dynamic spectrum allocation for efficient cognitive radio network management
PY  - 2026
T2  - Computer Networks
VL  - 281
C7  - 112217
DO  - 10.1016/j.comnet.2026.112217
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105033232520?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Electronics and Communication Engineering, Sri Venkateswara College of Engineering, Tamilnadu, Sriperumbudur, 602117, India
AB  - Cognitive Radio Networks (CRNs) can address spectrum scarcity, but real-world implementation still faces challenges such as severe dynamic traffic, incomplete and unreliable spectrum sensing, stringent QoS/URLLC demands, overhead from multi-agent collaboration, limited fairness among diverse users, energy constraints at the Internet of Things (IoT) edges, and jamming and adversarial vulnerability, especially in dense 5G-Advanced/6 G and in RIS-assisted environments. The existing issues are addressed by introducing the Reinforcement Learning-related Dynamic Spectrum Allocation (RL-DSA) framework, which is used to control, predict, and ensure the safety of CRN management. Initially, the hierarchical multi-agent RL controller receives context from the transfer-related occupancy forecaster with graph networks. The multi-agent controller performs fast power actions, which help ensure the long-horizon goal is effectively achieved. During the analysis, constrained RL is incorporated with this process to balance the equality between energy trade-offs. Then, adversarial training and federated meta-RL are applied to reduce the error and stabilize learning through scheduling. Finally, RL digital twins are applied to perform policy stress testing and explainability via the attention map, ensuring trust while allocating resources in the CRN topologies. The framework's efficiency is evaluated using experimental results, including collision probability, energy, and spectral efficiency. The effective results provide the path to regulate the DSA for next-generation CRNs.The proposed Reinforcement Learning-related Dynamic Spectrum Allocation (RL-DSA) framework enhances spectrum efficiency and supports robust cognitive radio network management, thereby contributing to SDG 9 (Industry, Innovation, and Infrastructure) by fostering sustainable and resilient communication infrastructure. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.
