TI  - Adaptive reconfigurable software-defined vehicles (ARSDV): a deep-reinforcement-learning-based approach for optimized smart vehicle drive
PY  - 2026
T2  - Journal of Supercomputing
VL  - 82
IS  - 12
C7  - 623
DO  - 10.1007/s11227-026-08767-0
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047027485?origin=resultslist
AD  - Department of Computer Engineering and Information Technology, Shiraz University of Technology, Shiraz, Iran
AB  - The automotive industry is undergoing a significant transformation toward software-defined vehicles (SDVs), in which software systems govern the driving experience, enabling vehicles to evolve beyond traditional mechanical functions. The adaptive reconfigurable SDV (ARSDV), introduced in this paper, is an SDV that autonomously adjusts or replaces its software modules based on current environmental, vehicular, traffic, and infrastructural conditions. The goal is to improve overall driving utility, measured in terms of safety, energy consumption, resource demand, and travel time. However, due to the wide variety of software module implementations provided by different vendors and third-party companies, each exhibiting different quality attributes, identifying the optimal software configuration for deployment on the vehicle’s hardware in a timely manner under current contextual and resource constraints remains a challenging task. Achieving this level of adaptability requires real-time decision-making under millisecond-level latency constraints and dynamic resource management on the vehicle’s centralized high-performance computing (HPC) platform to satisfy operational requirements. A blockchain-based repository is also employed as an immutable record of verified software modules used by the ARSDV reconfiguration algorithm. As conventional optimization methods fail to determine the optimal software reconfiguration in a high-dimensional, dynamic decision space within stringent real-time constraints, this study proposes a deep-reinforcement-learning (DRL)-based adaptive software reconfiguration approach for ARSDVs. Implemented using Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), the proposed DRL model directly learns the mapping between driving contexts and optimal software configurations. The DRL agent continuously interacts with the environment, observing factors such as road type and traffic conditions, and adaptively selects the configuration that maximizes long-term driving utility. Experiments in simulated urban scenarios were conducted within a digital-twin environment built on the Autoware platform and ROS2, encompassing diverse road topologies with randomized traffic densities. Results indicate that the proposed method achieved a 15% average improvement in total driving utility—defined as the integrated reward accounting for safety, energy consumption, resource demand, and travel time—compared to conventional SDVs, consistently attaining higher cumulative utility. © The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Science+Business Media, LLC, part of Springer Nature 2026.
