AB  - Antarctic wharf infrastructure forms a critical interface between polar maritime logistics and station-based scientific operations. Its planning, construction, operation, and decommissioning must address extreme ice conditions, permafrost degradation, short construction windows, limited equipment accessibility, and stringent environmental governance. This review proposes an integrated Environment–Structure–Equipment–Governance (ESEG) framework for conceptualising Antarctic wharves as coupled engineering systems rather than conventional port facilities. Within this framework, environmental processes define dynamic constraints, structural systems provide engineering responses, equipment and logistics determine constructability, and governance sets the boundaries of acceptable engineering activity. Three major transitions in Antarctic wharf engineering are identified. First, environmental-load assessment is shifting from deterministic design values towards probabilistic and coupled multi-hazard evaluation involving sea ice, icebergs, permafrost, wind, waves, and currents. Second, structural systems have evolved from consumable ice wharves and conventional piled or gravity-based facilities towards prefabricated modular systems with improved constructability, reversibility, and lifecycle adaptability. Third, construction practice is moving from site-intensive field operations towards factory-controlled prefabrication, digital pre-assembly, lean construction, and dedicated polar equipment support. Despite these advances, major knowledge gaps remain, including the absence of dedicated Antarctic wharf design standards, limited understanding of ice–structure–foundation coupling mechanisms, insufficient quantitative evidence on construction safety and human factors, and unresolved criteria for decommissioning and long-term ecological recovery. Future priorities include long-term field monitoring, probabilistic design, digital twins, sub-ice robotic inspection, in-situ resource utilisation, intelligent materials, low-carbon construction, and multilateral technical coordination. Representative projects, including Panda Wharf and Qinling Station, are further examined to illustrate transferable experience in modular construction, bolted connections, polar logistics, and governance-compatible infrastructure development. © 2026 Institution of Structural Engineers. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.
